{"t": 1794116443.515815, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "Heads-up: I fixed the red `contract-tests` check on this branch.\n\n**What failed**\n`contract/test_fee_contract.py::test_fee_rounding_contract` \u2014 `fee(2.675)` returned `2.67` instead of the expected `2.68`.\n\n**Why**\nWith binary floats, `2.675` is actually stored as `2.67499999...`, and `round()` also uses banker's rounding, so the last cent was dropped. The card-network contract (PAY-88) requires fees to be computed on exact decimal values and rounded HALF-UP to cents.\n\n**What I changed** (`payments/fees.py`)\n- Replaced the float calculation `round(amount * 0.01 * 100, 2)` with exact decimal arithmetic: `Decimal(str(amount)) * Decimal(\"0.01\") * 100`, quantized to cents with `ROUND_HALF_UP`, then converted back to `float`.\n- Kept the function signature and the 1% fee semantics unchanged.\n- Added a short docstring note pointing at PAY-88 so the float/decimal choice doesn't get reverted by accident.\n\n**Verified locally**\n- `fee(2.675) == 2.68` (the failing contract case)\n- `fee(0.125) == 0.13`, `fee(1.005) == 1.01`, `fee(2.665) == 2.67` (other half-up boundaries)\n- `fee(100.0) == 100.0` and the existing test suite still pass\n\nNote: keeping floats and only changing the rounding call won't fix this \u2014 the conversion to an exact decimal (`Decimal(str(amount))`) is what makes 2.675 round up.", "issue_number": 214, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
